Day 67/100 of #DrGViswanathanChallenge

Today, I solved LeetCode 173 – Binary Search Tree Iterator
The problem was about implementing an iterator that returns the elements of a BST in ascending order, i.e., inorder traversal.

- Approach
Instead of storing the entire inorder traversal, I used a stack to simulate recursion:
Push all left nodes onto the stack.
next() pops the smallest available node.
Then move to its right subtree and push its left chain.
hasNext() checks whether there are still nodes to process.
This gives us an efficient lazy inorder traversal.

Time complexity: O(1) 
Space complexity: O(h)

- Key Takeaways:
1) BST inorder traversal produces elements in sorted order.
2) A stack can effectively replace recursive traversal.
3) Iterators are useful when we don't want to store the entire traversal.

